      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="curve-range" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. The Non-Motorised Curve Range</h2>
<p>
            The Curve is the Woodway most gym-goers know: a self-powered treadmill whose running
            surface curves upward at front and back, so that stepping forward on the curve speeds the
            belt and drifting back slows it. There is no motor and no speed setting. Woodway lists
            five versions:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Curve.</strong> The original, with about 5% curvature, a 67 by 17-inch running surface, and 400 lb running and 800 lb walking ratings on Woodway's spec sheet. Its standard display needs no electricity.</li>
            <li><strong>Curve Trainer.</strong> A shallower curve of about 3%, which Woodway pitches at endurance running rather than sprinting.</li>
            <li><strong>Curve FTG.</strong> The Trainer's 3% curve with sled-pull straps and attachment points for resisted work.</li>
            <li><strong>Curve XL.</strong> A much bigger machine: 86 by 22-inch running surface and a 550 lb unit weight on Woodway's sheet.</li>
            <li><strong>Curve LTG.</strong> The lighter-duty, lower-priced model for homes and light commercial use, in the second card above.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Woodway's documents again disagree with themselves. The Curve spec sheet gives its unit
            weight as 355 lb in one place and 335 lb in another, and its 67-inch running length sits
            beside a metric figure of 142 cm, which is closer to 56 inches. Woodway's product page
            gives overall dimensions of 70 by 33 by 72 inches; the spec sheet gives 67 by 33 by 66.5.
            Ask for the figures that apply to the unit you are quoted.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Curve or motorised?</h3>
          <p>
            A curved manual treadmill is a different tool, not a cheaper version of a motorised one.
            It suits sprints, intervals and form work, because speed follows your effort instantly,
            and it needs no outlet. It is harder to hold a steady easy pace on, it has no incline, and
            TreadmillReviews.com notes that runners often go slower on one than on a motorised
            treadmill. If your plan is daily steady running, walking or incline walking, a motorised
            machine fits better. Our <a href="/best-curved-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">curved treadmill roundup</a>
            sets the Curve LTG against the AssaultRunner, SOLE, TrueForm and others.
          </p>
          <p>
            Price is the other difference. Woodway does not publish a price for the full Curve; Garage
            Gym Reviews describes it as a $5,000-plus treadmill, and other estimates run higher. The
            Curve LTG, at $3,495 on sale when we checked, is the only Curve Woodway prices openly.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. What a Woodway Costs, and Why</h2>
<p>
            These are the prices Woodway's own shop listed on the day we checked (20 Oct 2026). They
            change, they exclude delivery and tax, and most of the range is sold only by quote.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Listing</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Price on 20 Oct 2026</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Curve LTG (new)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$3,495 sale, $3,995 regular</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Vive (new)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$11,395</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4Front Quick Set (new)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$13,995 to $17,105</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4Front Personal Trainer (new)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$14,480 to $17,590</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4Front ProSmart (new)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$16,885 to $19,995</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4Front Certified Renewal</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$11,195</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4Front Certified Pre-Owned (2022 to 2025 units)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$11,100 to $14,950</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Within each 4Front version, the options are priced in steps: on the Quick Set, the 15 mph
            upgrade added $535, the 18 mph upgrade $2,160, the steeper incline packages $635 and
            reverse $315. A runner who wants 18 mph, a steeper incline and reverse on the touchscreen
            console is looking at $19,995 before delivery.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why so much</h3>
          <p>
            Part of the price is the mechanism. A slat surface with over a hundred bearings, toothed
            drive belts and an aluminium-and-rubber slat for every few inches of running length costs
            far more to build than a belt and a deck. Part is the build model: Woodway's FAQ says it
            builds to order, delivers by van line to the room, and that lead times vary. And part is
            the market. Woodway's FAQ says there is no difference between its home and commercial
            products; every treadmill meets the same commercial standard with the same grade of
            components. You are paying for a gym and laboratory machine whether or not you need one.
          </p>
          <p>
            The motor figure does not explain the price. The 4Front manual gives a 2 hp continuous
            (5 hp peak) brushless servo drive, which is a lower continuous figure than many $2,000
            home treadmills print on their labels. That is not a weakness: a slat surface on bearings
            needs less power to turn than a belt dragging over a deck, and brands rate motors in
            different ways. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a>
            explains why the label is a poor way to compare machines.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The price compared with the alternatives</h3>
          <p>
            For context, a premium conventional home treadmill with a heavy frame, a 22 by 60-inch
            deck and a long motor warranty typically costs between about $3,000 and $7,000. Landice's
            core L7, for instance, was listed from $5,429 when our Landice guide checked it on 14
            October. A 4Front
            costs two to four times as much. The case for paying that rests on lifespan, capacity and
            ride, not on features.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Warranty, Buy-Back and the Home-Use Question</h2>
<p>
            Woodway publishes one set of warranty terms per model rather than separate residential
            and commercial terms. Most of its spec sheets label them "Commercial Warranty", and its
            FAQ says home and commercial products are the same. In short:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>4Front, Mercury, Path, Pro and Ridgerunner:</strong> 5 years on drive, motor and belt; 3 years on all components; 1 year on labour.</li>
            <li><strong>Vive:</strong> 5 years on the frame, 3 years on parts, 1 year on labour.</li>
            <li><strong>Curve, Curve Trainer, Curve FTG and Curve XL:</strong> 5 years on frame and belt, 3 years on components, 1 year on labour.</li>
            <li><strong>Curve LTG:</strong> 5 years on the frame, 3 years on the belt and moving parts, 1 year on wear items and labour.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Those terms are commercial-grade, which is unusual to find on a home treadmill, and they
            would be generous on a gym machine. But a home buyer used to marketing for consumer
            treadmills should notice that they are shorter on paper than the lifetime frame or
            lifetime parts cover some cheaper brands offer. Landice, for example, gives lifetime parts
            on its residential L7 and L8. Woodway's argument is that its surface rarely needs a
            claim; ours is that you should read the terms for what they say. Our
            <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a>
            explains how to compare terms across brands.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Conditions that matter</h3>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Original purchaser only.</strong> The 4Front manual says the warranty is given only to the original purchaser. A privately bought used Woodway comes with no factory cover.</li>
            <li><strong>Maintenance records.</strong> The manual says the warranty does not apply if there is "a failure to provide maintenance procedure documentation" as the manual sets out. Keep a log from the first week.</li>
            <li><strong>Electrical codes.</strong> Damage from failing to comply with electrical codes is excluded, which makes the dedicated circuit a warranty issue as well as a practical one.</li>
            <li><strong>Where it is used.</strong> The manual forbids operating the treadmill outdoors or outside its stated temperature and humidity range.</li>
          </ul>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The buy-back guarantee</h3>
          <p>
            One term is unusual. Woodway's manual says it guarantees to repurchase its treadmills for
            up to five years after original installation, as a payment or as credit toward a new
            Woodway, based on the original purchase price, for the original owner, and only with proof
            of annual preventive maintenance. Woodway does not publish the percentage it pays, and
            says to contact it for the details and restrictions. If the buy-back is part of your
            reasoning, get the percentage in writing before you order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-requirements" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Power, Floor and Space: Putting a Woodway in a Home</h2>
<p>
            A Woodway arrives as commercial equipment and expects the house to meet commercial
            installation standards. Before ordering one, check four things.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Power</h3>
          <p>
            Every motorised Woodway needs its own circuit. The 4Front manual specifies a 115-volt
            20-amp supply on a NEMA 5-20R outlet with a dedicated 20-amp circuit, or 208/230 volts on a
            NEMA 6-20R outlet, and says no other treadmill or device may share the supply line.
            Woodway's FAQ adds that the circuit cannot share a neutral, and that machines with top
            speeds of 16.5 mph and above need the 208/230-volt supply. Most household outlets are
            15-amp, so for many homes this means an electrician and a new circuit before delivery.
            Never run one through an extension lead. The non-motorised Curve models need no outlet, apart from optional
            powered consoles.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Floor and weight</h3>
          <p>
            The 4Front weighs 445 lb before a runner steps on it, and the Pro and Ridgerunner more.
            The manual asks for a level, stable surface, with an extra base board if needed, says that
            on upper floors the treadmill should go as close to a corner as possible after the
            building structure has been checked, and warns that the floor's load capacity must exceed
            the machine's weight plus the dynamic load of a person running. It also says not to place
            it directly on thick carpet, because of moving parts underneath, and to use a mat.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Space and climate</h3>
          <p>
            The 4Front is about 72 inches long and 35 inches wide, and the manual asks for a clear
            safety zone of at least 40 by 80 inches (1 by 2 metres) behind it. Its stated operating
            range is 50 to 104°F with 20-95% non-condensing humidity, so an unheated garage in a
            northern winter is outside it. Running indoors is a requirement, not a suggestion: the
            manual forbids outdoor use.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Delivery</h3>
          <p>
            Woodway's FAQ says treadmills ship by van line to the exact location, and that stairs,
            disassembly and sites a tractor-trailer cannot reach are not included in freight rates.
            The manual says most Woodways arrive fully assembled. Measure every doorway, turn and
            stair on the route, tell Woodway about them when you ask for a quote, and get the
            delivered price in writing. Our guide to
            <a href="/gym-treadmill-for-home/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">putting a gym treadmill in a home</a>
            covers the same questions for other commercial brands.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="used-and-refurbished" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Buying a Used, Refurbished or Certified Pre-Owned Woodway</h2>
<p>
            The slat surface's long life makes used Woodways attractive, and there are three ways to
            buy one.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">From Woodway</h3>
          <p>
            Woodway runs two programmes. Its Certified Pre-Owned units are, in its words, a small
            selected percentage of treadmills resold after a multi-point inspection, with a two-year
            parts and one-year labour warranty, or the remainder of the original warranty depending
            on age. On 20 October 2026 its shop listed four Certified Pre-Owned 4Front Quick Set units,
            built between 2022 and 2025, at $11,100 to $14,950, and a 4Front Certified Renewal at
            $11,195. Those are not large discounts on a new $13,995 Quick Set. The 2025 pre-owned
            unit, listed as a standard 12.5 mph, 15% incline Quick Set, was priced at $14,950, above
            Woodway's own price for a new Quick Set in that specification. Compare the new and
            pre-owned listings line by line before choosing.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">From a remanufacturer</h3>
          <p>
            Independent dealers sell remanufactured units for less. On the same day Fitness Superstore
            listed a remanufactured 4Front with a 2024 Quick Set display at $10,199, a 4Front with a
            ProSmart 10-inch screen at $11,199 and a Desmo Evo at $6,299. Its listings describe a
            one-year parts and on-site labour warranty with paid extensions, and note that units may
            ship with used belts. Woodway's own listings say it does not authorise or endorse any
            secondary reseller, and that its slat-belt design needs service procedures and custom
            components that only Woodway produces. That does not make a remanufactured unit a bad
            buy, but it means your cover comes from the seller, not Woodway.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Privately</h3>
          <p>
            Private and auction listings, often ex-gym or ex-clinic, are cheapest and carry no
            warranty. Before paying:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Get the serial number and ask Woodway what model, year and options it was built with.</li>
            <li>Inspect the slats for cracks, missing rubber and damaged aluminium profiles, and listen for bearing noise at walking and running speeds.</li>
            <li>Ask for maintenance records, and whether the axle bearings have been greased annually.</li>
            <li>Check the electrical supply on the nameplate against your house; a 208/230-volt unit needs a different circuit.</li>
            <li>Price delivery separately. Moving a 400-plus-pound treadmill costs real money.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a>
            covers the general checks that apply to any second-hand machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Who Should Buy a Woodway, and Who Should Not</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy a 4Front if the treadmill is central to your training.</strong> If you run or
            walk on it most days, share it with other heavy users, want speeds up to 18 mph or steep
            incline, or simply want one machine for the next twenty years and can afford it, a 4Front
            is the most durable home treadmill we know of, on Woodway's ratings and its long record in
            gyms and labs.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Consider a Woodway if you are heavier than most treadmills allow.</strong> A
            400 lb running and 800 lb walking rating on the 4Front is far above typical home
            ratings, and Woodway backs them with the same frames it sells to gyms and hospitals.
            Cheaper machines rated to 400 lb exist, so compare before assuming only a Woodway will do.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the Curve LTG if you want a curved manual treadmill.</strong> It is the most
            affordable new Woodway and needs no outlet, but it is a sprint and interval machine, not a
            walking treadmill.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy something cheaper if your use is ordinary home use.</strong> Thirty to sixty
            minutes a day of walking and running does not need a commercial machine, and a premium
            conventional treadmill does that job for $3,000 to $7,000 with a longer warranty on paper.
            Our <a href="/landice-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Landice guide</a>
            covers one heavy American alternative, and the commercial-grade roundup linked below
            covers several more.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere if your house cannot take it.</strong> No dedicated 20-amp circuit,
            an upper-floor room you cannot check, an unheated garage or a narrow staircase are each a
            reason to stop, or to budget for fixing them before you order.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look elsewhere if you want built-in classes.</strong> Woodway's consoles are built
            for data and control, not streamed coaching, and the touchscreen console's cloud services
            carry a fee. If a trainer on a screen is what gets you moving, a screen-led machine suits
            you better.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where are Woodway treadmills made?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Woodway USA is based in Waukesha, Wisconsin, and builds its treadmills to order. The brand began in Germany in the 1970s, and Woodway says the American company was formed in 1990 after a US licence was granted to a Waukesha manufacturer in 1988. Woodway does not say on the pages we read where every component comes from.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does a Woodway treadmill cost?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On 20 October 2026 Woodway’s shop listed the non-motorised Curve LTG at $3,495 on sale, the Vive at $11,395 and the 4Front from $13,995 to $19,995 depending on console and options. Certified pre-owned 4Fronts were $11,100 to $14,950. Most other models are sold by quote. Prices change and exclude delivery.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a Woodway slat belt last?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Woodway rates its slat running surface for about 150,000 miles and says owners should not need to replace a belt or deck. That is the manufacturer’s rating rather than independent data. The axle bearings still need greasing once a year, and the drive belt needs checking every six months, according to the 4Front manual.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does Woodway make a home treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Woodway says there is no difference between its home and commercial treadmills: all are built to the same commercial standard. The Curve LTG is the model it positions most clearly for home gyms, and the Mercury and Vive are its more compact motorised options. Warranty terms are the same commercial-grade terms whether the machine goes into a house or a gym.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What electrical supply does a Woodway need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Motorised Woodways need a dedicated 20-amp circuit that shares no neutral. The 4Front runs on 115 volts with a NEMA 5-20R outlet, or 208/230 volts with a NEMA 6-20R outlet. Woodway’s FAQ says models with top speeds of 16.5 mph and above need 208/230 volts. The non-motorised Curve models need no outlet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a used Woodway treadmill worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Often, if the price reflects its age and condition. Woodway’s own certified pre-owned units carry a two-year parts and one-year labour warranty, but were priced close to new when we checked. Remanufactured units from dealers are cheaper with the seller’s warranty. A private sale carries no factory cover, because Woodway’s warranty belongs to the original purchaser.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes Buying a Woodway Treadmill</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">Woodway treadmills rarely fail in the ways cheaper machines do. These are the errors that cost buyers money, cover or a delivery day.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Plugging it into an ordinary household outlet</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A motorised Woodway needs a dedicated 20-amp circuit with a NEMA 5-20R outlet, or a 208/230-volt NEMA 6-20R supply on faster models, and damage from ignoring electrical codes is excluded from the warranty. Confirm the exact supply for your configuration with Woodway, and have an electrician install the circuit before the treadmill arrives.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not planning the delivery route</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A 4Front weighs 445 lb and usually arrives assembled, and Woodway’s freight rates exclude stairs, disassembly and hard-to-reach sites. Measure every doorway, turn and stair, send those measurements with your quote request, and get the delivered and installed price in writing before you pay.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the maintenance log</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Woodway’s manual says the warranty does not apply without maintenance documentation, and its buy-back guarantee depends on proof of annual preventive maintenance. Keep a dated record of every six-monthly check and the annual bearing greasing from the first month, or buy Woodway’s preventive maintenance programme.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying second-hand and assuming the warranty follows</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Woodway’s warranty is given only to the original purchaser, and Woodway says it does not authorise secondary resellers. A private or dealer used unit is covered only by what the seller offers. Get that warranty in writing, check the serial number with Woodway, and budget for a new belt if the unit ships with a worn one.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

